# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` checks every
# text file out as CRLF. That is harmless for the app, the DB setup and the
# flows — but NOT for the tests that treat `.sql` files as data and assert on
# their exact bytes:
#
#   * tests/guardrails/billing-customer-bill-line-replace-boundary.test.ts
#   * tests/guardrails/billrun-inventory-write-boundary.test.ts
#     Both strip SQL line comments with `line.replace(/--.*$/, "")` after
#     `split("\n")`. With CRLF, each line still ends in `\r` — and `\r` is a
#     JavaScript regex LINE TERMINATOR, so `.` never matches it and `$` (no `m`
#     flag) never matches before it. The strip silently no-ops, the read-only
#     boundary prose in `db/bootstrap/billrun-db-roles.sql` (which legitimately
#     names INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) survives into the assertion, and the
#     `not.toMatch(/\b(INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|TRUNCATE)\b/i)` guardrails fail.
#
#   * tests/pgledger/transform.test.ts
#     Compares the regenerated pgledger SQL to the committed file byte-for-byte.
#
# Pinning `.sql` to LF in the working tree — on every platform — makes those
# nine tests pass on Windows without weakening a single assertion.
#
# `* text=auto` only normalizes to LF *in the repository*; it does not change
# what any existing checkout looks like, so it is safe to add here.
* text=auto

# Data files whose exact bytes are asserted by tests — always LF on disk.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
